WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:06.000
The investigation provides P–S intervals of 0.25 s at White Sands,

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:11.540
0.45 s at Hillside, and 0.78 s at Mesaview.

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:14.960
White Sands is nearest; Mesaview is farthest.

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:18.860
Read each interval against this investigation’s own travel-time graph.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:24.820
It gives approximate distances of 2.5 km, 4.5 km, and 7.8

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:29.920
km respectively; these are source-specific graph readings, not a universal conversion.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:33.220
Use the map scale to draw those radii around the three

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:37.840
named stations. Their common region lies offshore near the trench, supporting

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:42.480
the trench-related earthquake interpretation rather than Lava Mountain or Red Mesa.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:48.500
Sandpoint stands on reclaimed loose coastal material, so tsunami exposure and

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:52.040
weak-ground behavior both matter. Riverton is low at the head of

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:56.580
a sandy bay with gentle offshore slopes, creating substantial inundation concern.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:00.760
Ashton lies on steep volcanic slopes with large blocks nearby;

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:02.580
shaking can trigger slope failure.

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:07.240
Cascade Village and Cliffside lie near fractured cliffs, and Cliffside also

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:11.160
has soft, wet ground. Mesaview is farther from the offshore source

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:13.760
but sits between steep Red Mesa and Lava Mountain.

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:17.620
Greater source distance can reduce shaking relative to nearer sites without

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:19.140
eliminating slope hazards.

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:24.080
Hillside occupies a higher inland setting with gentler adjacent slopes.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:28.080
In this scenario those features make it a defensible lower-hazard choice,

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:29.960
not a guarantee of no shaking.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:33.360
White Sands is behind a reef in the supplied scenario;

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:37.060
that is the course’s relative comparison with exposed Sandpoint.

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:40.820
A reef is not reliable real-world protection from a tsunami and

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:42.860
does not justify staying on the coast.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:47.000
Rank towns by the specified event and hazard, then cite elevation,

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:50.140
distance, sediment, slope, and coastal geometry.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:55.120
The textbook allows reasoned alternatives for its overall safest/least-safe ranking;

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.360
a single unlabeled risk score would conceal those assumptions.
